     "rows": [
      [
       "g = gcd(n, k)",
       "Number of independent groups",
       ""
      ],
      [
       "arr[start::g]",
       "All elements whose indices differ by a multiple of g",
       ""
      ],
      [
       "sorted(...); med = vals[len//2]",
       "Median of the group, which minimises the total change",
       ""
      ],
      [
       "sum(abs(v - med))",
       "Operations to make the whole group equal the median",
       ""
      ]
     ]
    }
   },
   {
    "h": "Walk through an example",
    "p": [
     "`arr = [1,4,1,3]`, `k = 2`. Here `n = 4`, so `g = gcd(4, 2) = 2`."
    ],
    "table": {
     "head": [
      "Group",
      "Elements",
      "Median",
      "Cost"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "start 0",
       "1, 1",
       "1",
       "0"
      ],
      [
       "start 1",
       "4, 3",
       "4",
       "1"
      ]
     ]
    }
   },
   {
    "h": "Why gcd(n, k)",
    "p": [
     "In a circular array, two windows of length k that start at neighbouring indexes have equal sums only if `arr[i] == arr[i + k]` (wrapping around). Repeating that rule links indexes that differ by multiples of k modulo n, which are exactly the classes modulo gcd(n, k). Each class must become one value, and the median is the cheapest."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Time and space",
    "bullets": [
     "Time: O(n log n).",
     "Space: O(n)."
    ]
   }
  ],
  "takeaway": "All window sums are equal exactly when each class of indexes modulo gcd(n, k) holds one value; make each class equal to its median."
 },
